    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d1365">

                <pb facs="1470" n="1456" sameAs="frus:frus1944v07/pg1456"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">103.9169/3241: Airgram</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Ambassador in Paraguay</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic">Frost</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic"
                        >to the Secretary of State
                    </hi>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Asunción</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1944-04-15T08:45:00-04:00">April 15,
                            1944—8:45 a.m.</date>
                        <lb/> [Received April 21—2 p.m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>A–123. For FEA,<anchor n="2" xml:id="d1365-f1"/> Crowley<anchor n="3"
                        xml:id="d1365-f2"/> &amp; Dodson.<anchor n="4" xml:id="d1365-f3"/> Reference
                    is made to circular airgram March 30, 1944, 7:30 p.m.,<anchor n="5"
                        xml:id="d1365-f4"/> regarding a farther rollback of decentralization to a
                    minimum positive list as of July 1st, 1944.</p>
                <p>In view of earlier removals of large groups of commodities from the
                    decentralization procedure<anchor n="6" xml:id="d1365-f5"/> and also the relaxed
                    control over shipments valued at less than 10,000 dollars announced by FEA, this
                    Mission believes that the use of a minimum positive list would be desirable; not
                    only because it implies removal of more commodities from decentralization, but
                    also because it will provide a more definite basis for determining in each case
                    whether an import recommendation is necessary or not.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Frost</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d1365-f1" n="2" xml:id="d1365-f1-content">
                <p>Foreign Economic Administration.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d1365-f2" n="3" xml:id="d1365-f2-content">
                <p>Leo T. Crowley, Foreign Economic Administrator.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d1365-f3" n="4" xml:id="d1365-f3-content">
                <p>Raymond I. Dodson, Assistant to the Director, Pan American Branch, Bureau of
                    Areas, Foreign Economic Administration.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d1365-f4" n="5" xml:id="d1365-f4-content">
                <p>Not printed.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d1365-f5" n="6" xml:id="d1365-f5-content">
                <p>For correspondence on the decentralization plan of lodging some of the export
                    controls In the countries of destination, see <ref target="frus1943v05#pg_106">
                        <hi rendition="#italic">Foreign Relations</hi>, 1943, vol. <hi
                            rendition="#small-caps">v</hi>, pp. 106</ref> ff.; see also the circular
                    airgram of February 15, 1944, <hi rendition="#italic">ante</hi>, <ref
                        target="#pg653">p. 653</ref>.</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
    </standOff>
